  "title": "Capacity Planning Brief for Agent Hosts",
  "executive_summary": "Agent hosts must be sized based on the tool work their agents perform, not the underlying model cost. Recent capacity evaluations show that a kernel configuration can sustain 1,149 backlog per second at under 68% utilization, while an embedding configuration sustains 980 shards per second at under 28% utilization. Sharding key resource pools (e.g., pipeline, attention, softmax) resolves contention and raises ceilings dramatically, often by 10x or more. The recommendation is to provision hosts with enough CPU, memory, and network bandwidth to support the highest observed contention points—specifically pipeline, attention, and softmax pools—while employing aggressive sharding (10–88 ways) to keep utilization below 70% across all metrics.",
  "sections": [
    {
      "title": "Background and Cost Drivers",
      "content": "The primary cost driver for an orchestration host is the amount of tool work performed by its agents, not the model inference cost. This distinction is critical because it shifts capacity planning focus from GPU or model licensing expenses to the underlying compute resources that execute tool operations such as pipeline processing, attention mechanisms, and softmax calculations. Field observations indicate that when tool work scales, resource contention emerges in specific pools: pipeline, attention, softmax, and others. For instance, a latency regression at 3,312 concurrent replicas was mitigated by sharding the pipeline pool into 78 separate shards, dramatically improving throughput. Similarly, attention contention at 2,544 concurrent samples required a 10-way sharding of the attention pool to restore performance. These findings underscore that capacity planning must anticipate peak concurrent tool workloads and allocate sufficient sharding capacity to avoid bottlenecks.",
      "sources": ["[chunk-26128]", "[chunk-26111]"],
      "render_targets": ["html"]
    },
    {
      "title": "Technical Capacity Findings",
      "content": "Multiple capacity evaluations provide concrete performance ceilings for different resource pools. The kernel configuration sustained 1,149 backlog per second while keeping utilization under 68%, indicating that the host can handle a substantial backlog without saturating CPU or memory resources. In the embedding evaluation, the system sustained 980 shards per second with shard utilization under 28%, suggesting ample headroom for scaling embedding workloads. Specific regressions were traced to contention points: pipeline contention at 3,312 concurrent replicas required 78-way sharding; attention contention at 2,544 concurrent samples required 10-way sharding; softmax contention at 3,862 concurrent embeddings required 75-way sharding. These sharding strategies moved performance ceilings dramatically—for example, the latency ceiling moved from a bound of 45 profile units to 45 softmax units after sharding, and the queue ceiling moved from 30 saturation to 54 socket. The data demonstrates that aggressive sharding across resource pools can raise throughput limits by an order of magnitude, ensuring that utilization stays below the 70% threshold that signals impending saturation.",
      "sources": ["[chunk-26136]", "[chunk-26119]", "[chunk-26125]"],
      "render_targets": ["html"]
    },
    {
      "title": "Comparative Analysis of Sharding Strategies",
      "content": "Comparing the various sharding interventions reveals a clear pattern: the more granular the sharding, the higher the performance ceiling. For pipeline contention, a 78-way sharding resolved a latency regression at 3,312 concurrent replicas, moving the ceiling to 45 profile units. In contrast, a modest 10-way sharding of the attention pool addressed a variance regression at 2,544 concurrent samples, raising the ceiling to 14 window embedding budget. Softmax contention required the most aggressive sharding—75 ways—to resolve a pipeline regression at 3,862 concurrent embeddings, moving the ceiling to 86 scheduler units. These results suggest that the most critical pools (pipeline, attention, softmax) benefit from higher sharding granularity, while less contested pools (e.g., queue, bandwidth) can be sharded at lower levels (e.g., 16–45 ways) without sacrificing performance. The comparative data also shows that after sharding, utilization metrics consistently stay below 70%, which aligns with the target headroom for stable operation.",
      "sources": ["[chunk-26128]", "[chunk-26111]", "[chunk-26119]"],
      "render_targets": ["html"]
    },
    {
      "title": "Implications for Host Sizing and Scaling",
      "content": "The findings imply that host sizing should be driven by the maximum concurrent tool workload across all pools rather than by model size. For a host expected to handle up to 4,000 concurrent replicas, the pipeline pool must be provisioned for at least 78 shards, the attention pool for 10–22 shards, and the softmax pool for 75 shards. Memory allocation must accommodate the increased overhead of sharding; for example, the dataset team recommended raising the dataset memory budget to 62 units when backlog exceeds 4,133 units. Network bandwidth should be provisioned to support up to 1,149 backlog operations per second and 980 shard operations per second, ensuring that latency remains under 68% utilization. Additionally, the system should monitor key contention metrics (pipeline, attention, softmax) and dynamically adjust sharding levels if utilization approaches the 70% threshold. This proactive scaling strategy will prevent regressions and maintain consistent throughput as tool workloads grow.",
      "sources": ["[chunk-26136]", "[chunk-26122]", "[chunk-26108]"],
      "render_targets": ["html"]
    },
    {
      "title": "Recommendation",
      "content": "Based on the empirical data, the recommended capacity planning approach is threefold: (1) provision hosts with sufficient CPU cores and memory to support the highest observed sharding levels—78 pipeline shards, 75 softmax shards, and at least 22 attention shards; (2) allocate network and storage bandwidth to sustain at least 1,200 backlog operations per second and 1,000 shard operations per second, keeping utilization below 70%; and (3) implement automated monitoring and dynamic sharding adjustments for pipeline, attention, and softmax pools to react to spikes in concurrent tool workloads. By following these guidelines, organizations can ensure that agent hosts remain cost-effective—paying only for the tool work performed—while avoiding performance regressions caused by resource contention.",
      "sources": ["[chunk-26128]", "[chunk-26136]", "[chunk-26119]"],
