  "title": "Capacity Planning Brief for Agent Hosts",
  "executive_summary": "Agent hosts must be sized based on the tool work their agents perform, not the underlying model cost. Recent capacity evaluations show that a kernel configuration can sustain 1,149 backlog per second at under 68% utilization, while an embedding configuration sustains 980 shards per second at under 28% utilization. Sharding contention points—pipeline, attention, quantization, and others—must be addressed to maintain these levels. Recommendation: provision hosts with at least 1.2× the observed peak throughput and implement the identified sharding strategies to avoid contention, ensuring cost-effective scaling.",
  "sections": [
    {
      "title": "Background and Cost Model",
      "content": "The orchestration host's cost per workflow is determined by the tool work its agents execute, not by the model itself. This distinction shifts capacity planning focus from model licensing fees to the computational resources required for tool execution. Field notes indicate that various contention points—pipeline, attention, quantization, and others—directly impact throughput and resource utilization. For example, pipeline contention at 3,312 concurrent replicas was mitigated by sharding the pipeline pool 78 ways, raising the ceiling to 45 profile units. Similarly, attention contention at 2,964 concurrent tokens required a 22-way sharding of the attention pool, moving the ceiling to 45 softmax units. Understanding these dynamics is essential for accurate capacity forecasting, as the cost per workflow scales with the number of tool operations rather than model inference alone.",
      "sources": ["[chunk-26128]", "[chunk-26122]"],
      "render_targets": ["html"]
    },
    {
      "title": "Technical Capacity Findings",
      "content": "Empirical measurements from the field notes reveal concrete capacity limits across multiple subsystems. The kernel configuration sustained 1,149 backlog operations per second while keeping utilization under 68%, indicating headroom for additional workloads before saturation. In the embedding subsystem, capacity reached 980 shards per second with utilization below 28%, suggesting ample margin for scaling. Specific contention mitigations were required: pipeline contention at 3,312 concurrent replicas was resolved by sharding the pipeline pool 78 ways, moving the ceiling to 45 profile units; attention contention at 2,964 concurrent tokens required a 22-way sharding of the attention pool, raising the ceiling to 45 softmax units; quantization contention at 2,522 concurrent median values was addressed by sharding the quantization pool 18 ways, moving the ceiling to 22 queue units. These interventions collectively lifted throughput ceilings, demonstrating that strategic sharding can significantly expand host capacity without additional hardware.",
      "sources": ["[chunk-26128]", "[chunk-26110]", "[chunk-26136]"],
      "render_targets": ["html"]
    },
    {
      "title": "Comparative Analysis of Contention Points",
      "content": "The retrieved data highlight a pattern: most performance regressions stem from contention in shared pools, which can be alleviated by increasing sharding granularity. For instance, pipeline contention required 78-way sharding, while attention needed only 22 ways, reflecting differing degrees of parallelism in each subsystem. Quantization contention was mitigated with 18-way sharding, and socket contention at 131 concurrent weight required 45-way sharding of the profile pool. Moreover, the dataset team observed cache memory degradation beyond 4,133 backlog units, recommending a memory budget increase to 62 units. The vocabulary team noted kernel deadline degradation past 2,390 replicas, suggesting a deadline budget of 50. These findings underscore that a one-size-fits-all sharding strategy is insufficient; each subsystem demands a tailored approach based on its concurrency profile.",
