      "render_targets": ["html"]
    },
    {
      "title": "Recommendation and Action Plan",
      "content": "To achieve cost‑effective scaling, we recommend the following steps: 1) Provision agent hosts with a baseline capacity of 1,400 backlog operations per second and 1,200 shard operations per second, providing a 20% headroom over observed peaks. 2) Apply the sharding strategies identified in the field notes: pipeline pool 78‑way, attention pool 22‑way, quantization pool 18‑way, profile pool 45‑way, and socket pool 45‑way. 3) Increase memory budgets as suggested—dataset memory to 62 units and attention latency to 39 units—to prevent degradation under high load. 4) Continuously monitor concurrency metrics (e.g., concurrent replicas, tokens, and backlog) and adjust sharding granularity dynamically. This approach ensures that host capacity aligns with the true cost driver—tool work—while maintaining performance and minimizing unnecessary expenditure.",
